Subject: [PATCH 0010] Start a dict's entries on a pointer boundary
Upstream: CPython 3.13.7
Upstream-Archive: https://www.python.org/ftp/python/3.13.7/Python-3.13.7.tgz
Upstream-SHA256: 6c9d80839cfa20024f34d9a6dd31ae2a9cd97ff5e980e969209746037a5153b2
Build-Variants: capstone64 domain (musl)
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

A dict keys object is a header, the hash index table, then the entries, and
the entries start at dk_indices + (1 << dk_log2_index_bytes). The smallest
dict (8 slots, 1-byte indices) has an 8-byte table, which is pointer-aligned
wherever a pointer is at most 8 bytes. On capstone64 a pointer is a 16-byte
capability that must be stored 16-aligned, so every entry of a small dict was
8 bytes off, and the first capability store into one trapped ("Unaligned cap
access", cause 4, in insert_to_emptydict, the first boot that got that far).

new_keys_object now pads the table up to the alignment of a pointer. The index
WIDTH is still chosen from dk_log2_size everywhere, so the extra bytes are
never read as indices. Where a pointer is at most 8 bytes the loop never runs.
A static assertion checks that the table itself starts on a pointer boundary.

--- a/Objects/dictobject.c
+++ b/Objects/dictobject.c
@@ -810,6 +810,17 @@
     else {
         log2_bytes = log2_size + 2;
     }
+    /* The entries start right after the index table, and they hold pointers,
+       so the table must end on a pointer boundary. Every platform with pointers
+       of at most 8 bytes gets that for free (the smallest table is 8 bytes);
+       where a pointer is a 16-byte capability the 8-byte table of the smallest
+       dict put every entry 8 bytes off, and the first capability store into it
+       trapped. The index width still follows log2_size; this only pads. */
+    while (((size_t)1 << log2_bytes) < _Alignof(PyObject *)) {
+        log2_bytes++;
+    }
+    static_assert(offsetof(PyDictKeysObject, dk_indices) % _Alignof(PyObject *) == 0,
+                  "the dict index table must start on a pointer boundary");
 
 #ifdef WITH_FREELISTS
     struct _Py_dictkeys_freelist *freelist = get_dictkeys_freelist();
